+// from them. In a no-copy buffer the GPU wrote its own copy, so those bytes go back into the caller's memory (unified memory
+// does that on an iPhone). Only the bytes the kernel writes: the rest of the buffer may already hold newer CPU data.
+// ponytail: luma kernels only (sizes from their AIR); a general no-copy write-back needs each kernel's write ranges.
+- (void)bgLuma:(BGMTLBuffer *)b offset:(NSUInteger)off length:(NSUInteger)len sum:(int)sum
+{
